Task 6: Create a Strong Password and Evaluate Its Strength
Objective

To understand what makes a password strong by creating and testing different passwords against online strength checkers, and to evaluate their resistance to common password attacks.

1. Passwords Created (Varying Complexity)

Simple: lemon123

Medium: BlueSky2025

Strong: Storm@2025

Very Strong: xT9$Lp3@Z1vW

2. Testing on Password Strength Checker

(Tool used: passwordmeter.com)

Password	Length	Complexity Used	Score / Feedback
lemon123	8	lowercase + numbers	Weak – Too short, predictable, dictionary-based
BlueSky2025	10	uppercase + lowercase + numbers	Fair – Better but still guessable (dictionary word + year)
Storm@2025	10	mixed case + numbers + symbol	Strong – Good complexity, resistant to dictionary attack
xT9$Lp3@Z1vW	12	upper + lower + numbers + symbols	Very Strong – Randomized, long, highly resistant to brute force
3. Best Practices Identified

Length matters → Aim for at least 12–16 characters.

Use variety → Combine uppercase, lowercase, numbers, and special characters.

Avoid patterns → Don’t use birthdays, names, or dictionary words.

Randomness is key → A password like xT9$Lp3@Z1vW is much harder to crack than BlueSky2025.

Passphrases work too → Example: H@ppy!Sunshine_2025 (easy to remember, but complex).

4. Common Password Attacks

Brute Force Attack: Tries every possible combination. Longer, more complex passwords take exponentially more time.

Dictionary Attack: Uses common words, names, and known weak passwords. Easily defeats short/simple passwords like lemon123.

5. Summary: How Complexity Affects Security

Simple passwords are cracked in seconds by brute force or dictionary attacks.

Medium complexity passwords may resist dictionary attacks but still fall quickly to brute force.

Strong passwords with mixed character types significantly increase resistance.

Very strong, random passwords (12+ chars, symbols, unpredictable) may take centuries or longer to crack with current technology.

6. Tips Learned

Always use 12+ characters.

Mix upper, lower, numbers, and symbols.

Avoid common words, names, or patterns.

Consider passphrases for memorability.

Regularly update and manage passwords using a password manager.

